From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 14:22:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 48/74] fix(tests): build pack test environments from an
 allowlist (gcp-9ur)

Seven tests in the pack suite passed on CI and failed for anyone running the
suite from inside a gastown agent session. CI's runner exports none of the
GC_*/BEADS_* namespace; every agent in this town does, and the affected tests
inherited it. Six failures came from GC_BIN -- the code under test reads
os.environ.get("GC_BIN", "gc") and the tests assert argv[0] == "gc", so an
exported GC_BIN=/Users/.../gc made argv[0] an absolute path -- and one from
GC_TEMPLATE, which leaked into the claim script's route computation and made it
reject the fixture route. The cost was not the red tests: four beads (gcp-ks5,
gcp-cvb, part of gcp-e6xv, plus a mayor consolidation pass) were filed against
a defect that never existed on the branch.

Two mechanisms produced it. The github and discord intake tests snapshot
os.environ in setUp, add GC_CITY_ROOT and restore on teardown -- preserving the
ambient namespace rather than clearing it -- and test_formula_assets.py built
subprocess environments as {**os.environ, ...} at fourteen sites. Both now go
through an isolated_environ() helper that starts from an explicit allowlist
(HOME, LANG, LC_ALL, PATH, TMPDIR) and layers the variables a test actually
means to set; the in-process cases apply it via mock.patch.dict(..., clear=True),
the precedent this same file already used at two call sites. Production code is
untouched on purpose -- honoring GC_BIN is correct behavior, and the defect is
entirely in test isolation.

The allowlist is deliberate rather than a pop-list of the two known-bad names,
and this commit removes the three pop-lists that were already in the tree
(GITHUB_INTAKE_* in one setUp, BEADS_ACTOR/GC_SESSION_*/GC_AGENT at two
subprocess sites): popping fixes today's variables and leaves the class intact,
so the next name an agent session gains recreates the bug. Each pack keeps its
own copy of the helper because packs ship independently from this repo and must
not import test infrastructure across pack boundaries.

tests/test_env_hermeticity.py is the part that makes it stick. It runs the three
affected modules twice as subprocesses -- once with a representative synthetic
agent session injected, once with the namespace stripped -- and asserts the
per-test outcomes are identical, so a new leak in any guarded module turns it
red regardless of which variable causes it. Verified both directions in this
session: red against the pre-fix tree, naming exactly the seven tests above, and
green after. Directory-level coverage was considered and rejected -- discord/tests
is 42s of unrelated socket tests, so the guard runs modules and costs ~35s.

The guard confirms a divergence by re-running just the candidates before
reporting. Two claim-command tests in test_formula_assets.py bound a subprocess
at two seconds and time out under machine load; that flake is pre-existing
(it reproduces on unmodified 3d92618) and is filed as gcp-eqh8, but without the
confirm round it would surface here as a phantom isolation regression -- the
same false signal this guard exists to prevent. Drop the confirm round when
gcp-eqh8 lands.

Verification: the full CI invocation over the eight test directories now reports
the same result with and without GC_BIN/GC_TEMPLATE set, which is the property
the bead asks for. The residual failures in both runs are test_no_bare_bd_commands
(gcp-xk96, unmerged) and gcp-eqh8's load flake; neither is this change's.
